Expand description

A chart showing values as [bars](struct.Bar.html "struct ratatui::widgets::Bar").

Here is a possible `BarChart` output.

```
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│                             ████│
│                        ▅▅▅▅ ████│
│            ▇▇▇▇        ████ ████│
│     ▄▄▄▄   ████ ████   ████ ████│
│▆10▆ █20█   █50█ █40█   █60█ █90█│
│ B1   B2     B1   B2     B1   B2 │
│ Group1      Group2      Group3  │
└─────────────────────────────────┘
```

A `BarChart` is composed of a set of [`Bar`](struct.Bar.html "struct ratatui::widgets::Bar") which can be set via [`BarChart::data`](struct.BarChart.html#method.data "method ratatui::widgets::BarChart::data"). Bars can be styled globally ([`BarChart::bar_style`](struct.BarChart.html#method.bar_style "method ratatui::widgets::BarChart::bar_style")) or individually ([`Bar::style`](struct.Bar.html#method.style "method ratatui::widgets::Bar::style")). There are other methods available to style even more precisely. See [`Bar`](struct.Bar.html "struct ratatui::widgets::Bar") to find out about each bar component.

The `BarChart` widget can also show groups of bars via [`BarGroup`](struct.BarGroup.html "struct ratatui::widgets::BarGroup"). A [`BarGroup`](struct.BarGroup.html "struct ratatui::widgets::BarGroup") is a set of [`Bar`](struct.Bar.html "struct ratatui::widgets::Bar"), multiple can be added to a `BarChart` using [`BarChart::data`](struct.BarChart.html#method.data "method ratatui::widgets::BarChart::data") multiple time as demonstrated in the example below.

The chart can have a [`Direction`](../prelude/enum.Direction.html "enum ratatui::prelude::Direction") (by default the bars are [`Vertical`](../prelude/enum.Direction.html#variant.Vertical "variant ratatui::prelude::Direction::Vertical")). This is set using [`BarChart::direction`](struct.BarChart.html#method.direction "method ratatui::widgets::BarChart::direction").

Note: this is the only widget that doesn’t implement `Widget` for `&T` because the current implementation modifies the internal state of self. This will be fixed in the future.

## [§](#examples)Examples

The following example creates a `BarChart` with two groups of bars. The first group is added by an array slice (`&[(&str, u64)]`). The second group is added by a [`BarGroup`](struct.BarGroup.html "struct ratatui::widgets::BarGroup") instance.

```
use ratatui::style::{Style, Stylize};
use ratatui::widgets::{Bar, BarChart, BarGroup, Block};

BarChart::default()
    .block(Block::bordered().title("BarChart"))
    .bar_width(3)
    .bar_gap(1)
    .group_gap(3)
    .bar_style(Style::new().yellow().on_red())
    .value_style(Style::new().red().bold())
    .label_style(Style::new().white())
    .data(&[("A0", 0), ("A1", 2), ("A2", 4), ("A3", 3)])
    .data(BarGroup::new([
        Bar::with_label("B0", 10),
        Bar::with_label("B2", 20),
    ]))
    .max(4);
```

For simpler usages, you can also create a `BarChart` simply by

```
use ratatui::widgets::{Bar, BarChart};

BarChart::new([Bar::with_label("A", 10), Bar::with_label("B", 20)]);
```

